      answer: 1,
      why: '"The goal is, briefly, to grasp the native\'s point of view": immersion, language competence and daily-life observation replaced armchair synthesis from traders\' and missionaries\' reports — founding participant observation as a discipline-defining method.',
    },
    {
      q: 'An emic account describes a ritual as "honouring the ancestors"; an etic account describes it as "a redistribution of wealth enforcing reciprocity networks." What is the relationship between them?',
      choices: ['The etic is wrong', 'They are complementary: insider meaning (emic) and analyst-level functional description (etic) answer different questions', 'The emic is scientific, the etic is not', 'They are the same thing'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The emic view captures what the practice means to participants; the etic view places it in comparative, analytical terms. Ethnography needs both — meaning without comparison is parochial; comparison without meaning is hollow.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does fieldwork conventionally last a year or more?',
      choices: ['Visas require it', 'To observe a full annual cycle of activities (seasons, rituals, harvests, budget cycles) and to move past initial performance of the guest role', 'University rules', 'It takes that long to learn to take notes'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'A full cycle catches what a short stay misses (the dry season, the funeral that comes once a year), and trust and ordinary behaviour emerge only after the initial scaffolding of hosting a guest collapses.',
    },
    {
      q: 'The Yanomami controversy (Tierney\'s 2000 book alleging harms by researchers) is taught as the case study for what lesson?',
      choices: ['Fieldwork should be shorter', 'Research ethics in anthropology must treat communities as partners — with review, reciprocity and accountability — not as subjects', 'Genetic research should be banned', 'Ethnographers cannot study conflict'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Whatever the book\'s own accuracy, the firestorm forced the discipline to confront power: who consents, who benefits, who is accountable, and who can review or veto research about them — the modern community-partnership standard grew from that reckoning.',
    },
  ],
};

